@anon.c8777b812d6b35c09a8e6156f259107b.90 = private unnamed_addr constant <{ [2203 x i8] }> <{ [2203 x i8] c"The keys to consider when caching builds for the project.\0A\0ACache keys enable you to specify the files or directories that should trigger a rebuild when\0Amodified. By default, uv will rebuild a project whenever the `pyproject.toml`, `setup.py`,\0Aor `setup.cfg` files in the project directory are modified, or if a `src` directory is\0Aadded or removed, i.e.:\0A\0A```toml\0Acache-keys = [{ file = \22pyproject.toml\22 }, { file = \22setup.py\22 }, { file = \22setup.cfg\22 }, { dir = \22src\22 }]\0A```\0A\0AAs an example: if a project uses dynamic metadata to read its dependencies from a\0A`requirements.txt` file, you can specify `cache-keys = [{ file = \22requirements.txt\22 }, { file = \22pyproject.toml\22 }]`\0Ato ensure that the project is rebuilt whenever the `requirements.txt` file is modified (in\0Aaddition to watching the `pyproject.toml`).\0A\0AGlobs are supported, following the syntax of the [`glob`](https://docs.rs/glob/0.3.1/glob/struct.Pattern.html)\0Acrate. For example, to invalidate the cache whenever a `.toml` file in the project directory\0Aor any of its subdirectories is modified, you can specify `cache-keys = [{ file = \22**/*.toml\22 }]`.\0ANote that the use of globs can be expensive, as uv may need to walk the filesystem to\0Adetermine whether any files have changed.\0A\0ACache keys can also include version control information. For example, if a project uses\0A`setuptools_scm` to read its version from a Git commit, you can specify `cache-keys = [{ git = { commit = true }, { file = \22pyproject.toml\22 }]`\0Ato include the current Git commit hash in the cache key (in addition to the\0A`pyproject.toml`). Git tags are also supported via `cache-keys = [{ git = { commit = true, tags = true } }]`.\0A\0ACache keys can also include environment variables. For example, if a project relies on\0A`MACOSX_DEPLOYMENT_TARGET` or other environment variables to determine its behavior, you can\0Aspecify `cache-keys = [{ env = \22MACOSX_DEPLOYMENT_TARGET\22 }]` to invalidate the cache\0Awhenever the environment variable changes.\0A\0ACache keys only affect the project defined by the `pyproject.toml` in which they're\0Aspecified (as opposed to, e.g., affecting all members in a workspace), and all paths and\0Aglobs are interpreted as relative to the project directory." }>, align 1
